<h2>The lifecycle of the deposit</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>1. Fixation.</strong> At the outset — and by supplementary orders as the case grows — the tribunal estimates its costs: fees, administrative expenses, venue, transcription, experts it appoints. In institutional arbitrations (such as those administered in Delhi by arbitral institutions) the schedule of the institution typically governs; in ad hoc references, the fee framework under the Act and the agreement of the parties set the frame.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>2. Call and shares.</strong> The deposit is called in equal shares — an interim allocation only. The final costs award under the Section 31A regime decides who ultimately bears what, usually following the event.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>3. Default handling.</strong> On one party's default, the other may pay the defaulter's share; on total default for a claim or counterclaim, the tribunal may suspend or terminate the proceedings for it.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>4. Accounting.</strong> On termination of the proceedings, Section 38(3) requires the tribunal to render an accounting of the deposits received and return any unexpended balance.</div>
</div>

<h2>Default as strategy — and why it backfires</h2>
<p>A respondent who believes the arbitration should never have begun is sometimes tempted to starve it: refuse the deposit and let the reference stall. The Act anticipates the move. The claimant can pay the respondent's share and press on, with the outlay recoverable in the costs award; the arbitration proceeds, and the defaulting party has purchased nothing but a line item against itself. The reverse manoeuvre — a claimant who will not fund its own claim — meets the harder edge: suspension or termination of the claim. Counterclaims obey the same logic independently, so a respondent funding its counterclaim cannot ride on the claimant's deposit, and the tribunal can terminate one side's case while the other proceeds.</p>

<div class="note">
  <p><strong>Where the deposit meets unequal parties:</strong> deposit calls can press hard on a smaller party locked in arbitration with a well-resourced opponent. The practical mitigations are unglamorous but real — seeking staged or supplementary deposits aligned to actual hearing phases, proposing institution-administered fee schedules at the appointment stage, and remembering that the equal-share rule is only an advance: a party confident on the merits is funding a recovery, not a loss. What a party should never do is ignore a call and assume the tribunal will carry on.</p>
</div>

<h2>The lien, and the court's check on it</h2>
<p>Section 39 closes the loop at the end of the case. The tribunal need not hand over the award while its costs are unpaid — the lien — and no party can force delivery except through the Section 39(2) mechanism: deposit the demanded costs in court, take the award, and let the court inquire into what the tribunal is properly owed, with any excess refunded. The design protects both interests: arbitrators are not left chasing fees after releasing the instrument that ends their mandate, and parties are not held hostage to a fee demand they dispute, because a judicial check exists. In practice, the provision matters most in ad hoc arbitrations without an institutional schedule; parties can largely design the problem away at the start by fixing the fee basis in the procedural order and keeping deposits current.</p>

<h2>Checklist for parties</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p>At commencement: get the fee and deposit framework recorded in the first procedural order, including the treatment of counterclaims and supplementary calls. During the reference: pay calls on time; if the opponent defaults, decide promptly whether to pay its share — delay suspends your own claim's progress. If cash-flow genuinely prevents payment, apply to the tribunal for staging before the due date rather than defaulting silently. At the end: demand the Section 38(3) accounting, and if a fee dispute blocks delivery of the award, use Section 39(2) rather than letting the limitation period for post-award remedies drift while the award sits undelivered.</p>
</div>

<div class="note">
  <p><strong>Note:</strong> this explainer is for general information only and is not legal advice.</p>
</div>
